Q: mingw32-make fails with Error 1 on Windows even though gcc works manually A: <p>Update:**</p>
<p>Solved. The issue is actually you can't use git bash for this. By just switching to MSYS2 Bash in VS code works perfectly in this case(editing setting.json by search Profile Windows). Now i only need deal error in my source code(i will try figured myself, so don't worry). Thanks especially to @Tim Roberts !!!</p>
<p>here is what changed:</p>
<pre><code>{
.

&quot;C_Cpp.default.compilerPath&quot;: &quot;C:\\msys64\\ucrt64\\bin\\gcc.exe&quot;,   &lt;-changed

...

&quot;terminal.integrated.profiles.windows&quot;: {...

    &quot;MSYS2 Bash&quot;: {      &lt;- added

            &quot;path&quot;: &quot;C:\\msys64\\usr\\bin\\bash.exe&quot;,

            &quot;args&quot;: [

                &quot;--login&quot;,

                &quot;-i&quot;

            ],

            &quot;env&quot;: {

                &quot;MSYSTEM&quot;: &quot;UCRT64&quot;,

                &quot;CHERE_INVOKING&quot;: &quot;1&quot;

            }

        }

    ...

},

&quot;terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.windows&quot;: &quot;MSYS2 Bash&quot;,

...

    &quot;terminal.integrated.env.windows&quot;: {

        &quot;PATH&quot;: &quot;C:\\msys64\\ucrt64\\bin;C:\\msys64\\usr\\bin;${env:PATH}&quot;

    },

...

}
</code></pre>

Q: EntityFramework OwnedType with Soft-delete A: <p>Would be more generalized solution - supports multiple levels of owned objects and not requires interface for owned types:<br />
<strong>Somewhere in your interceptor.cs</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>foreach (EntityEntry&lt;TYourSoftDeleteableInterface&gt; softDeletableEntry in softDeletableEntires)
{
    softDeletableEntry.State = EntityState.Modified;
    var entity = softDeletableEntry.Entity;
    entity.Delete(DateTime.UtcNow);

    foreach (var owned in softDeletableEntry.GetAllOwnedEntries())
    {
        owned.State = EntityState.Unchanged;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>OwnedTypeExtesions.cs</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static class OwnedTypeExtesions
{
    public static IEnumerable&lt;EntityEntry&gt; GetAllOwnedEntries(this EntityEntry from)
    {
        var owneds = from.References
                .Where(x =&gt; x.TargetEntry != null)
                .Select(x =&gt; x.TargetEntry)
                .Where(x =&gt; x.Metadata.IsOwned());

        return [..owneds, ..owneds.SelectMany(x =&gt; x.GetAllOwnedEntries())];
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Assign default read-write cosmos db role by terraform A: <p>The solution I found was to create a custom role like so:</p>
<pre><code>resource  &quot;azurerm_cosmosdb_sql_role_definition&quot;  &quot;cosmosdb_readwrite_role&quot; {
      name  =  &quot;CosmosDBReadWriteRole&quot;
      resource_group_name  =  azurerm_resource_group.resource_group.name
      account_name  =  azurerm_cosmosdb_account.example.name
      type  =  &quot;CustomRole&quot;
      assignable_scopes  =  [azurerm_cosmosdb_account.example.id]
      permissions {
        data_actions  =  [
            &quot;Microsoft.DocumentDB/databaseAccounts/readMetadata&quot;,
            &quot;Microsoft.DocumentDB/databaseAccounts/sqlDatabases/containers/items/*&quot;,
            &quot;Microsoft.DocumentDB/databaseAccounts/sqlDatabases/containers/*&quot;
    ]
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And then assign that role to the managed identity:</p>
<pre><code>resource  &quot;azurerm_cosmosdb_sql_role_assignment&quot; &quot;service_identity_cosmos_db_sql_assignment&quot; {
    resource_group_name  =  azurerm_resource_group.resource_group.name
      account_name  =  azurerm_cosmosdb_account.example.name
      role_definition_id  =  azurerm_cosmosdb_sql_role_definition.cosmosdb_readwrite_role.id
      principal_id  =  azurerm_user_assigned_identity.service_identity.principal_id
      scope  =  azurerm_cosmosdb_account.example.id
}
</code></pre>
